# WYSIWYG Editor

This repo is a **reusable React + TypeScript editor library**, not an application. Downstream apps import the package; they do not copy source.

Layout: `src/` (library), `playground/` (consumer app), `*.stories.tsx` next to public UI, `*.test.ts(x)` next to modules.

## Custom contenteditable

Build on native `contenteditable`. Do **not** add TipTap, Lexical, Slate, Quill, or Draft.js. Hide browser quirks in core (selection, commands, document model). Features must not poke the DOM ad hoc.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — feature reaches into the DOM
el.innerHTML = `<b>${text}</b>`

// ✅ GOOD — feature goes through core commands
editor.commands.toggleMark('bold')
```

## Visual HTML output

When serializing content from visual/WYSIWYG (`contenteditable`) editing, put presentation on inline `style` attributes in the result HTML. Keep structural tags (`p`, `ul`, `h1`, `a`, etc.). Do not emit editor CSS classes or rely on stylesheet rules for document formatting.

This applies to **document HTML** (`value` / visual-surface output), not editor chrome (toolbar and menus still use CSS modules).

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD — class-based / stylesheet-dependent formatting -->
<p class="align-center"><span class="text-red">Hello</span></p>

<!-- ✅ GOOD — inline styles in the serialized HTML -->
<p style="text-align: center"><span style="color: red">Hello</span></p>
```

## Modular + public API

Each feature (bold, lists, history, toolbar) is an isolated module with its own commands, UI, stories, and tests. Compose via the core command/plugin API. Do not import another module's internals.

Export a narrow public API from `src/index.ts`. `react` / `react-dom` are peer dependencies. Playground-only code must not leak into the library.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — leaking internals
export * from './core/selection-impl'

// ✅ GOOD — stable surface
export { Editor, EditorToolbar } from './components'
export type { EditorProps, EditorCommand } from './types'
```

## Storybook, playground, tests

- **Storybook:** every public component/module gets stories for isolated behavior. Stories are the visual contract, not the playground.
- **Playground:** `playground/` consumes the library the same way other projects will. Manual integration only — no editor logic lives here. When a public feature changes, update `README.md` and playground documentation (see the documentation rule).
- **Tests:** colocate unit tests with modules. Cover commands, selection, and serialization. Playground and Storybook do not replace tests.

## React / TypeScript

Functional components, strict TypeScript, no `any` on public types. Put editor logic in custom hooks, not class components.
